<title>USA: G7 to sharpen focus on EMU at future talks -Waigel.</title>
<headline>G7 to sharpen focus on EMU at future talks -Waigel.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-09-28</dateline>
<text>
<p>German Finance Minister Theo Waigel said he expected discussions and updates on European Economic and Monetary Union to form a standard part of Group of Seven discussions in future.</p>
<p>Waigel told a briefing following a meeting with top finance officials from the other G7 nations -- Britain, Canada, France, Italy, Japan and the United States -- that the non-Europeans had shown considerable interest in EMU.</p>
<p>&quot;I am certain that this will continue to be discussed at future G7 meetings,&quot; he said. &quot;There is a large need for information on this outside of Europe.&quot;</p>
<p>Waigel added that the G7 in general had agreed that the strict economic conditions laid down for monetary union in the Maastricht Treaty should not be weakened, as this could lead to the creation of a weak and unstable European currency.</p>
<p>&quot;The G7 supported a strict adherence to the criteria,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>European countries which want to join a single currency have to meet certain economic conditions next year. But most nations are finding difficulty in reining in their public budget deficits in time.</p>
<p>--Janet Northcote, Washington Newsroom +1 202 898 8310</p>
